    Complete beginner should use Bluehost for the easiest WordPress setup. Some technical comfort means SiteGround for
    the best value. Hands-off management points to WP Engine or Kinsta. Full developer control means DigitalOcean or
    Cloudways.
  </p>

  <h3>By Budget</h3>

  <p>
    Under five bucks monthly means SiteGround, A2 Hosting, or Bluehost. Fifteen to twenty-five monthly means Cloudways
    or Flywheel. Twenty-five to fifty monthly means WP Engine or Kinsta. Enterprise level means WP Engine Growth or
    Kinsta Business.
  </p>

  <h3>By Site Type</h3>

  <p>
    WordPress blog should use SiteGround or Bluehost. WordPress business should use WP Engine or Kinsta. <a
      href="/tools/best-ecommerce-hosting">E-commerce</a>
    should use Kinsta or Cloudways. Agency or multiple sites should use Flywheel or WP Engine. Custom applications
    should use Cloudways or DigitalOcean.
  </p>

  <h3>Our Recommendation</h3>

  <p>
    Start with value. SiteGround offers quality hosting at shared prices. Upgrade for growth by moving to WP Engine or
    Kinsta when traffic justifies it. Consider managed early because the time savings often exceed the cost difference.
    Use free migrations and don't let your current host trap you. Switching is easy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="migration">Migrating to a New Host</h2>

  <p>Switching hosts is easier than you might think. Here's the process.</p>

  <p>
    Step one is signing up with the new host. Create your account with the new host. Don't cancel your old hosting yet.
    You'll run both briefly during transition.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step two is requesting migration. Most quality hosts offer free migration. WP Engine, SiteGround, Kinsta, and
    Cloudways all <a href="/tools/migrate-website-without-downtime">migrate sites for free</a>. Provide your old host
    login details and they handle the technical work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step three is testing on the new server. Your new host provides a temporary URL to test your site before going live.
    Check all pages, forms, and functionality. Fix any issues before switching DNS.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step four is updating DNS. Point your domain to the new server by updating DNS records. Your host provides the
    correct values. DNS propagation takes twenty-four to forty-eight hours. Some visitors will see the old site during
    this window.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step five is verifying and canceling the old host. Once DNS has propagated and everything works, you can cancel your
    old hosting. Keep backups of the old site just in case.
  </p>

